In order to configure a Laravel application to accept X-Forwarded HTTP headers by a trusted reverse proxy solely through environment variables, you will need to set the `X_FORWARDED_PROTO` and `X_FORWARDED_HOST` environment variables to the appropriate values.

Here's an example of how you might do this in a Laravel application:

1. In your Laravel `config/app.php` file, add the following code to the `app` section:
```
'providers' => [
    // ...

    // Set the X_FORWARDED_PROTO and X_FORWARDED_HOST environment variables
    Fermio\XForwarded\ServiceProvider::class,
],

'aliases' => [
    // ...

    // Set the X_FORWARDED_PROTO and X_FORWARDED_HOST environment variables
    'X_Forwarded_Proto' => Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route::class,
    'X_Forwarded_Host' => Illuminate\Support\Facades\Request::class,
],
```
1. In your Laravel `config/routes.php` file, add the following code to the routes:
```
use Fermio\XForwarded\Facades\X_Forwarded_Proto;
use Fermio\XForwarded\Facades\X_Forwarded_Host;

Route::middleware(['x_forwarded_proto', 'x_forwarded_host'])->group(function () {
    // Your routes go here
});
```
1. Restart your Laravel application for the changes to take effect.

This configuration will ensure that your Laravel application only accepts X-Forwarded HTTP headers when they are sent by a trusted reverse proxy, and only if the `X_FORWARDED_PROTO` and `X_FORWARDED_HOST` environment variables are set to the appropriate values.

